==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*per-|*weyd-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|purveu||proviso}}, from {{der|en|xno|purveuest||it is provided}}, or {{m|fro|purveu que||provided that}} (statutory language), from {{der|en|fro|porveu||provided}}, past participle of {{m|fro|porveoir||to provide}}, from {{der|en|la|prōvideō}} (See [[provide]]). Influenced by {{m|en|view}} and its etymological antecedants.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} The [[enact]]ing part of a [[statute]].
# {{lb|en|legal}} The [[scope]] of a statute.
# Scope or [[range]] of interest or [[control]].
#: {{syn|en|responsibility}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=The Right of the Convention to Frame such a Constitution|title=[[w:The Federalist|The Federalist]]: A Commentary on the Constitution of the United States|page=255|year=1788|author=w:James Madison|passage=Will it be said that the fundamental principles of the Confederation were not within the '''purview''' of the convention, and ought not to have been varied?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|title=Logics of Conversation|page=7|author=Nicholas Asher; Alex Lascarides|passage=Rhetorical relations have truth conditional effects that contribute to meaning but lie outside the '''purview''' of compositional semantics.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2018-04-25|author=w:Ron Nixon|title=Scandals and Investigations, but Few Arrests, for Air Marshals Program|journal=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/25/us/politics/air-marshals-scandals-investigations.html|passage=Several air marshals have asked Congress to remove the program from T.S.A.’s '''purview''' and entrust it to a different agency, like Customs and Border Protection or the Federal Bureau of Investigation.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.vanityfair.com/style/2022/06/how-love-island-became-a-tv-reality-of-sex-fame-and-sometimes-tragedy|title=Inside ‘Love Island,’ From the Tragic Suicide Deaths to New Mental Health Protocols|date=2022-06-02|author=Anna Peele|work=Vanity Fair|passage=It was 2015 when Jain—whose '''purview''' includes commissioning new series, acquiring the rights to foreign hits, and publicly answering for any perceived misdeeds of ITV—set out to overhaul the tepidly received ''Celebrity Love Island'', a 12-person British popularity contest and ostensible matchmaker that disappeared after two seasons.}}
# [[range|Range]] of understanding.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|title=The Problem of China|page=18|author=Bertrand Russell|passage=Our company were noisy, gay, quarrelsome, full of facile theories, with glib explanations of everything, persuaded that there is nothing they could not understand and no human destiny outside the '''purview''' of their system.}}

